};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/artificial-intelligence/intermediate/classification-and-regression">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Classification and regression are <strong>supervised learning's two core tasks</strong>:
    predicting categories (spam/not-spam, will-churn/won't) and predicting quantities (price,
    demand, risk). Most business value from ML lives here — and most ML mistakes live in their
    evaluation metrics, chosen carelessly. You already know the skeleton from the
    foundational tier: a model is a function fitted to labelled examples. This page is where
    that skeleton gets muscles — the loss functions that define "wrong", the families of
    models that chase them, and the evaluation machinery that separates a model that memorises
    from one that generalises.
  </p>
  <p>
    The unifying frame: you have a dataset of pairs (input, correct answer) — an apartment's
    size and its sale price, an email and its spam label. The learner searches through a space
    of candidate functions for the one with the lowest average <strong>loss</strong> on that
    data. Everything else — trees, linear models, deep networks — is a choice of search space.
    Everything that follows about evaluation exists because <em>low loss on the training data
    is not the goal</em>; low loss on data the model has never seen is.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the task types and the metrics that don't lie" widget="equationStep" config={clsCfg} />

  <h2>What "learning from examples" actually optimises</h2>
  <p>
    The loss function is the contract between you and the algorithm: it defines what "wrong"
    means numerically. For regression the standard is <strong>squared error</strong> — the
    average of (prediction − truth)² — summarised as RMSE (root-mean-square error, the
    square root of that average, in the units of the target) or MAE (mean absolute error).
    Squaring punishes large misses disproportionately: an error of 10 costs 100 times an
    error of 1, not 10 times. For classification the standard is <strong>log loss</strong>
    (cross-entropy): −[y·log(p) + (1−y)·log(1−p)], where p is the model's stated probability
    of the true class. It is gentle when the model is right and confident, and brutally
    punitive when the model is confidently wrong — predicting 0.999 probability on a false
    claim costs about 6.9 log-units, the natural logarithm of 1000.
  </p>
  <p>
    These are not arbitrary conventions. Least squares goes back to Legendre (1805) and
    Gauss, who used it to recover the orbit of the asteroid Ceres from a handful of
    observations — the same mathematics, fitting a line, that a neural network's final layer
    uses today. And both losses fall out of maximum-likelihood reasoning: squared error is
    what you get if you assume your errors are Gaussian; log loss is what you get if you
    take the model's probability claims seriously. Change what you believe about the noise,
    and the loss you should minimise changes with it.
  </p>

  <h2>Regression: fitting the line and judging it honestly</h2>
  <p>
    Linear regression assumes the target is a weighted sum of the inputs: price ≈ w×area + b.
    Take eight apartments (floor area in thousands of square feet, price in $1000s):
    (0.5, 120), (0.8, 150), (1.0, 155), (1.2, 220), (1.5, 240), (1.8, 295), (2.1, 320) and
    (2.4, 360). Before any model, establish the <strong>naive baseline</strong>: predict the
    mean price (232.5) every time. Its RMSE works out to about $82k — exactly the standard
    deviation of the prices. Any model that cannot beat $82k is literally worse than
    ignoring the input.
  </p>
  <p>
    Least squares minimises the squared vertical gaps between line and points. Doing the
    algebra (slope = Σ(x−x̄)(y−ȳ) ⁄ Σ(x−x̄)²) gives slope ≈ 132 and intercept ≈ 47: price ≈
    132×area + 47, or about $132 per square foot. Its RMSE is about $11k, roughly a
    seven-fold improvement — and the coefficient of determination R² = 1 − (fit's squared
    error) ⁄ (baseline's squared error) ≈ 0.98, meaning the line explains 98% of the price
    variance. Drag the sliders below and watch how badly the fit degrades for slope or
    intercept off their optimum — the least-squares line is the bottom of a smooth bowl.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="fit the line by hand — the data wants slope ≈ 132, intercept ≈ 47" widget="plotter" config={fitCfg} />

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> R² or RMSE alone tells you if a model is good. They only
  tell you how it compares — to a baseline, or to another model on the same data. A model
  with RMSE $11k is excellent for house prices and useless for stock returns; a "high R²"
  on training data is exactly what overfitting produces. Every metric only means something
  relative to something.</p>

  <h2>Classification: from scores to probabilities</h2>
  <p>
    For categories, the simplest serious model is <strong>logistic regression</strong>:
    compute a linear score z = w·x + b, then squash it through the sigmoid function
    σ(z) = 1 ⁄ (1 + e<sup>−z</sup>), which maps any number into the interval (0, 1) — a
    probability. A score of 0 becomes 0.5; strongly positive scores approach 1; strongly
    negative approach 0. Training minimises log loss, so the model is rewarded for
    probabilities that are honest, not just for getting the side of 0.5 right.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why not just fit a line to 0/1 labels? Because a line happily predicts −0.3 or 1.7,
    which are not probabilities, and it handles the pile-up of examples at the extremes
    badly. The sigmoid is not decorative; it is what makes the outputs interpretable as
    chances. Also note the naming trap: despite "regression" in its name, logistic
    regression is a <strong>classifier</strong> — it is linear regression's sibling,
    repurposed through the sigmoid for categorical targets.
  </p>
  <p>
    The probability then meets a <strong>threshold</strong> to become a decision. At 0.5,
    an email claiming 0.51 spam is delivered to the junk folder; move the threshold to 0.9
    and only near-certain spam gets filtered, at the cost of more spam in your inbox. The
    model outputs a number; the operating point is a choice — and as we will see, that
    choice belongs to the business, not the data scientist.
  </p>

  <h2>Flexibility: underfitting, overfitting and cross-validation</h2>
  <p>
    A straight line through the apartment data may be too stiff — perhaps prices bend near
    the city centre. A <strong>decision tree</strong> offers more flexibility: it splits the
    data by questions ("area above 1.4k sq ft?"), each region getting its own constant
    prediction. Give it enough depth and it can hit every training point exactly — every
    apartment priced perfectly, including the oddball ones that are odd for reasons no
    future apartment will share. That is <strong>overfitting</strong>: near-zero training
    error, poor error on new data, because the model has memorised noise. The mirror-image
    failure is <strong>underfitting</strong>: a model too stiff to capture real structure.
    The third possibility — and the most dangerous in practice — is fitting noise that
    happens to correlate with the target in your sample: a data-leakage variety, like a
    "sales prediction" model that has accidentally learned the week the sale was recorded.
  </p>
  <p>
    The defence is honest evaluation. Split the data: train on one part, test on data the
    model never saw. Because a single split is noisy and wastes data, standard practice is
    <strong>k-fold cross-validation</strong>: divide the data into k parts (k = 5 or 10),
    train k times, each time holding out a different part as the test set, and average the
    k scores. The result estimates how the model will behave on the data you actually care
    about — the data that does not exist yet. Model selection — choosing depth, penalties,
    which features to include — is done on validation performance, never on training
    performance, and the final number is reported on a test set touched exactly once.
  </p>

  <h2>The accuracy trap: precision and recall</h2>
  <p>
    Now the famous failure. Suppose 100,000 card transactions cross your system daily and
    0.1% are fraudulent — 100 frauds. A "model" that always says "not fraud" scores
    99,900 ⁄ 100,000 = <strong>99.9% accuracy</strong> and catches nothing. Accuracy is
    the wrong lens when classes are imbalanced; you need the confusion matrix's four
    numbers: true positives (TP), false positives (FP), true negatives (TN), false
    negatives (FN). From it:
    <strong>precision</strong> = TP ⁄ (TP + FP) — when we raise an alarm, how often are we
    right? — and <strong>recall</strong> = TP ⁄ (TP + FN) — of all real fraud, what
    fraction did we catch?
  </p>
  <p>
    Worked example: our detector flags 500 transactions today; 80 are genuine fraud.
    Precision = 80 ⁄ 500 = 16% (each alarm is wrong 5 times in 6 — annoying for customers).
    Recall = 80 ⁄ 100 = 80% (we caught most of the fraud). The F1 score, the harmonic mean
    of the two, is 2 × (0.16 × 0.80) ⁄ (0.16 + 0.80) ≈ 0.27 — low, because the harmonic
    mean punishes whichever of the two is weakest. Different deployments weight the pair
    differently: cancer screening wants maximum recall (a missed tumour is catastrophic; a
    follow-up scan is not), while an automatic account-freeze system wants high precision
    (freezing a paying customer wrongly is expensive). There is no single "best" number —
    only a best number <em>for a cost structure</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>ROC curves: every threshold at once</h2>
  <p>
    A model that outputs scores can be evaluated at every threshold simultaneously. Each
    threshold yields one point: the true-positive rate (recall) against the false-positive
    rate (fraction of innocents wrongly flagged). Sweeping the threshold from strict to
    lax traces the <strong>ROC curve</strong>. A useless model traces the diagonal; a
    perfect one hugs the top-left corner. The <strong>AUC</strong> — area under that curve —
    has an elegant interpretation: the probability that a randomly chosen fraudulent
    transaction receives a higher score than a randomly chosen legitimate one. 0.5 is coin
    flipping; 0.9 means the model orders the two correctly 9 times in 10.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> AUC 0.9 means 90% accurate. It does not — AUC is
  threshold-free and accuracy is threshold-specific; the two can move in opposite
  directions. On heavily imbalanced problems, the precision-recall curve (precision against
  recall across thresholds) is usually the more informative view, because the ROC curve's
  true-negative count is dominated by the enormous negative class and flatters the model.</p>

  <h2>Thresholds are policy, calibration is honesty</h2>
  <p>
    Setting the operating threshold is an economics problem. If a missed fraud costs
    $1,000 and each false alarm costs $10 in customer friction, you happily accept a
    hundred false alarms to catch one more fraud — move the threshold down until the
    marginal trade stops paying. The data scientist's deliverable is a well-calibrated
    score; the threshold belongs to whoever owns the costs. <strong>Calibration</strong>
    itself is a property worth checking: if the model says "80% spam", roughly 80% of such
    emails should actually be spam. Models trained on hard-focused losses can be
    systematically overconfident or underconfident; reliability curves (predicted
    probability against observed frequency) expose it, and recalibration methods fix it.
    A miscalibrated model quietly corrupts every downstream threshold that trusts it.
  </p>
  <p>
    One more professional judgement: interpretability scales with stakes. Linear
    regression hands you its reasoning in its coefficients; a random forest does not, but
    attribution tools (SHAP values, which distribute each prediction's departure from
    average across its input features) can explain individual decisions. For credit
    decisions and medicine, the ability to explain is not optional — regulators (adverse
    action notices under fair-lending law in the US, explanation rights under GDPR in
    Europe) require it. A movie recommender can stay a black box; a parole model cannot.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The bias–variance decomposition.</strong> The expected squared error of a model
    on new data splits exactly into three parts: bias² (the systematic error from the model
    family being too stiff, the same on every sample), variance (the error from the model
    being too sensitive to which particular sample it drew — retrain on a new sample and
    the predictions move a lot), and irreducible noise. Linear regression on curved data:
    high bias, low variance. A deep tree: low bias, high variance. Regularisation — L1/L2
    penalties on weights, tree depth limits, early stopping — is the deliberate purchase of
    a little bias to retire a lot of variance. This single trade-off organises nearly every
    practical modelling decision you will make.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Learning curves as diagnosis.</strong> Plot test error against training-set
    size. Curves that have flattened close together signal a bias-limited model — more data
    will not help; you need a more flexible model or better features. A wide gap between
    training and test error that narrows as data grows signals a variance-limited model —
    more data genuinely helps. Before buying data or GPUs, look at the curve; it usually
    tells you which one you need.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Imbalanced data, handled properly.</strong> The toolkit, in order of preference:
    reweight the loss (a fraud costs the loss function as if it were 100 examples), move the
    decision threshold, resample (oversynthesise the minority with care — SMOTE-style
    interpolation can manufacture unrealistic examples), and evaluate with metrics immune to
    base rates. What you should almost never do is report accuracy on a 0.1% problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open problems.</strong> Data leakage remains the leading cause of inflated
    published results — information from the future or from the label sneaking into
    features (the "salary prediction" model that includes last year's bonus). Distribution
    shift breaks models silently: a fraud detector trained on 2023 fraud faces fraudsters
    who adapt, and unlike a broken bridge, a broken model looks exactly like a working one
    until someone checks. Monitoring is therefore part of the model, not an afterthought.
    And the field's quiet irony: competition-winning ensembles of many models rarely
    survive contact with production, where a slightly worse but simpler, faster, calibrated
    model is usually the right answer.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Hastie, Tibshirani &amp; Friedman, Elements of Statistical Learning; Géron, Hands-On Machine Learning; Molnar, Interpretable Machine Learning (free online); Russell &amp; Norvig, Artificial Intelligence: A Modern Approach, ch. 19; Chollet, Deep Learning with Python.</p>
</TopicPage>
